From: xPDFSDK team <noreply@xpdfsdk.local>
Date: 2026-08-03
Subject: [EXPERIMENT r25-normal-exp-0001] Size image decode from transform

Base
====
r25-8-0138 ordered stroke renderer tape. This is an isolated normal-page
experiment and is not part of the accepted r25 ship sequence.

Root cause
==========
CPDF_ImageRenderer currently asks CPDF_DIB to decode every image for the full
render-device width and height. A normal page image that occupies only part of
a preview therefore often misses the existing DCT/JPX reduced-resolution
decoder path and is decoded at a higher resolution than its transformed
destination can consume.

Invariant
=========
Decode demand is the image's source-axis footprint after its image-to-device
transform, bounded by the canonical device-sized demand. Invalid or degenerate
geometry requests {0, 0}, which preserves PDFium's existing full-resolution,
fail-closed behavior. The same destination demand applies to the image's
explicit or soft mask.

Mechanism
=========
Use CFX_Matrix::GetXUnit()/GetYUnit() to compute the transformed source-axis
lengths, round each outward, and pass that size through the existing loader and
resolution-aware page image cache. DCT and JPX can then reduce work before
pixel allocation; unsupported codecs retain their canonical full decode.

This adds no classifier, cache, persistent allocation, synchronization,
threading, page threshold, or document-specific policy.

